         DIGITAL Selected as Supplier of Windows NT Solution
        for New Trading Floor at New York Mercantile Exchange


            Digital PCs Running Windows NT Are Chosen for
             Mission-Critical Trading Floor Workstations


MAYNARD, Mass., March 17, 1997 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced the New York Mercantile (NYMEX) has purchased more 
than a 1,000 Digital Venturis PCs running Microsoft Windows NT for 
NYMEX's mission-critical trading floor workstations. Together with 
Pioneer Standard Electronics, Inc., a major Digital reseller, and 
Microsoft Corporation, Digital will provide the infrastructure for 
the trading floor at NYMEX's new building, the quarter following its 
opening scheduled for May.  Sold through Pioneer, the contract is 
worth approximately $5 million. 

     "We depend on systems from Digital to provide reliable 
computing power at every level of our computing architecture," said 
Allen Feryus, senior vice president of Information Services and 
Chief Information Officer at NYMEX. "We selected Digital's Alpha 
platforms for our enterprise applications in 1993, and just recently 
adopted Digital's Intel-based systems for our trader workstations 
and middle-tier servers. What's more, Digital is now our single
source for service end-to-end."

     Under the terms of the contract, Digital is providing Venturis 
PCs and Digital Prioris servers running Windows NT for NYMEX's 
administrative offices.  The sale also included Digital Multivendor 
Customer Services and Digital GIGAswitches to provide disaster 
recovery services. 

     NYMEX uses the 64-bit Digital OpenVMS operating system software 
running on a disaster-tolerant AlphaServer cluster to support their 
core trading and settlement applications. Clearing 21 and the Trade 
Management System (TMS) applications developed by NYMEX, as well as 
Windows NT running on Digital Venturis PCs, will be implemented the 
quarter following their move to the new facility.  Both the Clearing 
21 and TMS applications employ a unique three-tier client server 
architecture and take advantage of the fault-tolerant features of 
Digital's OpenVMS software, as well as the cost-effective features 
of Windows NT. 

     "We originally chose the combination of Digital OpenVMS and 
Windows NT because of the architectural similarity between the two 
operating systems," said Feryus.  "Now that the Microsoft and 
Digital Alliance for Enterprise Computing has formalized the 
relationship between the two companies, we have even more confidence 
in our choice," he added.

     The traders in the trading pits of NYMEX thrive in the complex 
world of futures and options hedging, position-taking and 
speculation, executing some 60,000 trades daily.  To compete in 
today's trading environment, brokers need access to the best tools 
available for sorting, analyzing, and executing trades.  To keep its 
leading position in this highly competitive environment, NYMEX must 
instantly respond to global markets, second by second, or trading
customers will simply trade elsewhere.  Traders who move half a million
barrels of oil in a single contract may lose their vital edge, not to 
mention millions of dollars, if transactions are delayed.  For NYMEX, 
replacing their IBM and Tandem systems with Digital AlphaServer systems 
running OpenVMS and Windows NT for their enterprise and Windows NT on 
the trading floor will bring greater flexibility for fast, reliable 
deployment of these leading-edge applications. 

     NYMEX, currently with headquarters at the World Trade Center in 
