           DIGITAL Revises Performance Standards for HMOs


MAYNARD, Mass., March 24, 1997 -- Continuing a commitment to 
providing its employees high quality, managed health care, Digital 
Equipment Corporation has released the "Third Edition of Digital's 
HMO Performance Standards."  The Standards -- designed to clarify 
expectations for HMOs participating in the Digital network and to 
improve the quality provided to Digital's HMO members -- were 
released to approximately 85 health care providers and partners 
across the United States.

     The "Third Edition of Digital's HMO Performance Standards" are 
an enhancement to the original version released in January 1991, 
with a second edition following in 1995.  Key highlights of the 
third edition include requirements that HMOs:

     o 	continue to move toward the provision of an Organized 
     	System of Care (OSC).
     o 	develop comprehensive demand management programs which
       	emphasize prevention and wellness.
     o 	develop an Information Technology strategy to enhance 
       	coordination of care.
     o 	focus on member satisfaction.
     o 	embark on initiatives to integrate behavioral health and 
       	primary care.
     o 	work toward full National Committee for Quality Assurance
       	(NCQA) accreditation.

     There are six key areas of competence against which Digital's 
HMO partners are measured, including a new section stressing the 
importance of Health Education and Preventive Services. These are:


     o 	Access and Member Services
     o 	Clinical Quality
     o 	Behavioral Health
     o 	Health Education and Preventive Services
     o 	Information Management
     o 	Finance and Contracts


     This most recent edition of the HMO Performance Standards 
incorporates information gleaned from a review of literature on 
current trends in health care practice and delivery, including the 
most recent NCQA, Health Plan and Employer Data Information Set 
(HEDIS) and Foundation for Accountability (FACCT) Standards and 
reporting requirements. The Standards are also based on an analysis 
of Digital's HMO partners' performance against the 1995 Digital HMO 
Performance Standards.  They were developed with the assistance of 
Digital's HMO Network Manager, UNICARE (formerly John Hancock).  

     "The third edition of the HMO Performance Standards fulfills 
two primary purposes," said Fran Bastien, Digital's U.S. manager of 
Health and Welfare Benefits.  "They set HMO performance expectations 
by establishing benchmarks for excellence from health care industry 
'best practices.'  Expectations are purposefully set at high levels 
to encourage health plans to continuously improve the quality and 
efficiency of services delivered.

     "And when used in the context of continuous quality 
improvement, the Standards promote performance consistency among a 
diverse network of for-profit, not-for-profit, staff, group, mixed 
and IPA model health plans," Bastien added.  "For national employers 
like Digital, consistent quality in the delivery of benefits to 
employees and their dependents is critical".  

     "Digital management is acting very responsibly by clearly 
articulating their expectations for health plan performance," said 
Dr. Mary Jane England, president of the Washington Business Group on 
Health.  "At the same time, they are encouraging health plan 
innovation and leadership on tough issues like the coordinated 
delivery of primary and behavioral health care, and the appropriate 
use of computerized information systems to manage patient care and 
the entire health plan."

     Digital currently offers its employees a total of more than 80 
HMOs nationwide, including 30 POS options.  More than 22,000 
employees and their families, which represents more than 80% of 
Digital U.S. population, are currently enrolled in an HMO option.  
"In addition to improving the quality of health care services 
offered to employees and their family members, this managed care 
strategy has saved Digital more than $100 million in comparison to 
the cost of traditional indemnity coverage since 1991," said 
Bastien.  

     Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/
server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide 
information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha and Intel platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.  
