               Digital Offers Free WEB Browser Plugin
               to Record and Send "Open" Voice E-Mail


     Lets Internet Users Send and Receive Voice E-Mail as Easily
                           as Text E-Mail


MAYNARD, Mass., March 6, 1997 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
announced today the release of the DIGITAL Voice Plugin, a free 
browser plugin that allows users to record and send voice e-mail 
directly from a Web page.  Recipients can listen to Voice Plugin 
messages with the most popular e-mail and audio applications.  No 
additional software is necessary.  The use of open standards ensures 
that Voice Plugin messages will be enjoyed by the widest possible 
audience.

     Because it functions as an element of a Web page, rather than a 
separate application, Web publishers can place the Voice Plugin 
wherever they want feedback.  This invites the user to "speak their 
mind" about what they see, immediately, with a simple, intuitive, 
graphical user interface that makes talking much easier than typing. 
The e-mail address and other parameters can be customized by the Web 
page.  As a result, recording and sending a complete message can 
take as little as three mouse clicks.

     A free download of the Voice Plugin is available at 
http://interface.digital.com/voice, and is expected to be offered on 
free/shareware Web sites shortly.  Users will require the following:

     *	Browser:  Netscape Navigator or Gold 3.xx
     *	Operating System:  Microsoft Windows 95 or Windows NT 4.0
     *	Hardware:  Intel PC with sound card and microphone
     *	Network:  SMTP server (for sending e-mail)

     Support for Microsoft Internet Explorer and Netscape 
Communicator is planned for the next release, scheduled to become 
available in the next few months.

     "The Voice Plugin is going to change the way you communicate," 
according to Maura Welch, columnist for The Boston Globe. "It's the 
one and only way to record and send voice e-mail directly from a Web 
page.  If you've been to any of the Cybersmith Cafis, you may already 
know about it, because DIGITAL debuted the technology at Cybersmith."

     The Voice Plugin was designed through the Speech Interaction 
Project at DIGITAL's Cambridge Research Laboratory (CRL) in 
Massachusetts. "This is part of a larger strategy for DIGITAL," said 
Chris Weikart, primary developer.  "Our goal is to build an infra-
structure for deploying speech applications over the World Wide Web."  
In addition, the group intends to enlist Voice Plugin users in an 
effort to build the world's first Web-speech database, an essential 
first step towards deploying speech across the Web.

     Dr. Robert Iannucci, CRL's director, sees the Voice Plugin as a 
significant step toward the widespread deployment of next generation 
Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) technologies. "DIGITAL is studying 
and prototyping human-computer interfaces for the 21st century.  HCI 
research is inherently experimental, and must be proven by real 
users as the solution to real-world problems.  The Internet is the 
platform of choice, and our release of the Voice Plugin is the first 
step in a series of research-related HCI projects on the Web.  It 
also opens the door to a world of opportunities in Web-based voice 
applications."  Research prototypes from CRL combining a number of HCI 
technologies (including the Voice Plugin) will be demonstrated at Spring 
Internet World in Los Angeles, Calif., during the week of March 12-14, 1997.

     The Cambridge Research Laboratory (CRL) is one of four 
laboratories of DIGITAL's Corporate Research and Advanced 
Development organization. With a dual focus on human-computer 
interaction and scalable computing, CRL's current research topics 
include vision-based interaction, speech interaction, video and 
image processing, HCI systems, multimedia indexing, mobile/wireless 
systems, and future computing systems.

